      "text": "We can see that we have $$n\\equiv 3\\pmod 4,\\qquad n\\equiv 2\\pmod 5$$\nModulus 5 equations imply that the only possible remainders of $n$ when divided by 5 are 2 and 3, implying that the difference between the two cases (i.e., modulo 5 minus modulo 4) is also 1. The difference 1 implies that the answer is either 5+1=6 or 4+1=5. $\\sqrt5$ is less than 6.5, meaning that there is no solution when the answer is 6 and if 6 is excluded, the smallest positive value of $n = \\boxed{7}$ by the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion. The answer is 7",
