      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "In this problem we want to minimize the amount of steps or in other words $n$ so we go about it different ways. The process of minimizing must be considered in two directions.\n\nWhen Ray starts on step 0 he will climb 1 step and be on step 1. He will then climb 3 more steps making a total of 4 steps. (4 + 3 = 7) This corresponds to one multiple of 4. If he had 1 more step it would now be where he started from which is a multiple of 4. This means we can say that $n$ will be $3$, $7$ or $11$. This still leaves us with an open end.\n\nWhen he starts on step 1 he will climb 1 step making a total of two steps. Then he will climb 3 steps making a total of four steps. Eventually he will be on 1 more step. He will climb 1 more step, 3 steps, 7 steps, 11 steps, 15 steps...and so forth. This gives us a pattern to look at. If we add each step in the pattern mentioned above we obtain.\n\\[1 + 3 + 4 + 11 + 15\\ldots\\]\n\nIf we solve this series we obtain that $41$. This is the first step where someone would realize the steps go up by increments of 12 then another increment. This means that we have a limited set of $n$ and in this case it is $14$, $22$ or $32$.\n\nNow we use a similar approach with 5. If we start on step 1 we climb to 2 straight up, from there we can see that we will climb 5, 10, 15 and so forth until it is lesser than 10 in value. This leads us to $8$ values so far.\n\nIf we use $5$ and have Ray start on step $2$ we climb 3, then 8, then 13, 20, 25 and so forth. Now we have $16$ options left.\n\nIf we use $5$ and start on step $3$ we know that you will come up to step $6$ then 11, $16$, $21$, $26$. Now we have 32 options left.\n\nSince we have already found that $41$ was the smallest value for the pattern that we saw we know that $8$ was the smallest answer. Since we already found $6$ and two a lower we can say that the smallest answer over $10$ is $6$.",
